=== layout: section

# Intro to BioTech Ethics

=== layout: subsection

# 1. Ethics and Morals

=== layout: bookend

# 1. Questions

@body

- How do personal morals and professional ethics differ? Give an example where they conflict.
- Which historical case changed ethical expectations in science?
- When has a discovery challenged a moral belief?
- Compare duties in law, medicine, biotechnology, and information technology.
- What can an ethics committee decide? What still depends on a scientist's personal judgment?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 13.

=== layout: two-panels

# 1. A Personal Belief and a Professional Duty

@left

*Personal morals*

A lawyer may believe a client did something wrong.

@right

*Professional ethics*

The client still deserves a defense. Representing a client does not mean endorsing their actions.

@citation

[ABA, Model Rule 1.2(b)](https://www.americanbar.org/groups/professional_responsibility/publications/model_rules_of_professional_conduct/rule_1_2_scope_of_representation_allocation_of_authority_between_client_lawyer/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 1. A Decision an Ethics Committee Can Review

@body

- A researcher wants to reuse donated blood for a new study.
- Ask what the donors agreed to, who bears the risk, and who gets the benefit.
- Would your answer change if donors could never be identified?

@citation

[HHS, Coded specimens in research](https://www.hhs.gov/ohrp/coded-private-information-or-biospecimens-used-research.html)  |  [The Belmont Report (1979)](https://www.hhs.gov/ohrp/regulations-and-policy/belmont-report/read-the-belmont-report/index.html)

=== layout: one-panel

# 1. One Blood Sample, Three Obligations

@body

A patient agrees to give blood for a diabetes study. Years later, a team wants to study ancestry.

- *Respect for persons:* What did the patient understand and choose?
- *Beneficence:* What could the new study reveal about the patient or their relatives?
- *Justice:* Who supplies the samples, and who shares in the benefits?

@citation

[The Belmont Report (1979)](https://www.hhs.gov/ohrp/regulations-and-policy/belmont-report/read-the-belmont-report/index.html)

=== layout: one-panel

# 1. Who Holds the Key?

@body

The sample label says A17. A hospital file connects A17 to a patient's name.

- If the new team can get that file, the code offers limited privacy.
- If the team cannot readily identify the donor, the U.S. research rules can apply differently.
- The original promise to donors still matters. What did the consent form say?

@citation

[HHS, Coded specimens in research](https://www.hhs.gov/ohrp/coded-private-information-or-biospecimens-used-research.html)

