=== layout: one-panel

# 1. Who Knew Treatment Was Available?

@body

From 1932 to 1972, the U.S. Public Health Service studied Black men in Tuskegee, Alabama, without obtaining their informed consent. Investigators withheld available treatment from participants with syphilis.

- Once penicillin was widely available, what duty did the researchers owe the participants?
- What would it mean to earn trust from families familiar with this history?

@citation

[CDC, The untreated syphilis study at Tuskegee](https://www.cdc.gov/tuskegee/about/index.html)  |  [The Belmont Report (1979)](https://www.hhs.gov/ohrp/regulations-and-policy/belmont-report/read-the-belmont-report/index.html)

=== layout: subsection

# 2. Public Perception

=== layout: bookend

# 2. Questions

@body

Two concerns raised in the original prompt are lack of trust and limited knowledge of the science.

- How do these two perceptions affect biotechnology? What can we do to improve the public's perception? What are some of the difficulties in predicting possible dangers of biotechnology?
- How have historical events shaped public trust in biotechnology? How might the media's portrayal of biotech developments impact public perception? Can you provide examples of how misinformation has influenced public opinion on biotech? What role do educational initiatives play in bridging the gap between scientific knowledge and public understanding?
- Can public perception influence funding and policy decisions in biotechnology?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 15.

=== layout: one-panel

# 2. Why People Disagree About Biotechnology

@body

- Someone may want more information, distrust the source, or disagree about who bears the risk.
- Who gives the information and what they disclose affect trust.
- People can agree about the biology and disagree about ownership, risk, or fairness.
- A headline can turn an early result into a claim that feels certain.

@citation

[National Academies, Communicating Science Effectively](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23674/chapter/4)

=== layout: two-panels

# 2. Two Headlines, One Result

@left

*What the experiment showed*

"Crop survived disease in a greenhouse trial."

@right

*What a headline claimed*

"New crop will end crop losses."

What would a grower need to see in the field?

@citation

[National Academies, Communicating Science Effectively](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23674/chapter/4)

=== layout: one-panel

# 2. A Neighbor Asks About the Greenhouse Result

@body

"The crop survived" answers one question. A neighbor asks what will happen to the insects in her field.

- Show what the researchers counted in the greenhouse.
- Ask which insects and seasons matter to her. That answer changes what a field study should measure.

@citation

[National Academies, Communicating Science Effectively](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23674/chapter/4)

=== layout: one-panel

# 2. What Would Earn Your Trust?

@body

Imagine two teams publish the same crop result. One posts the comparison data. The other asks growers to trust a press release.

- Which result can another grower check?
- Both teams may be right about the plants. The difference is what a grower can verify before planting.

@citation

[National Academies, Communicating Science Effectively](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23674/chapter/4)

=== layout: subsection

# 3. Scientific Misconduct

=== layout: bookend

# 3. Questions

@body

- What are the main forms of scientific misconduct as defined by the National Science Foundation (NSF)? How do data fabrication and falsification manifest in research, and what are their impacts?
- What are the motivators for scientists to commit misconduct? Is scientific misconduct more prevalent in certain fields of research? If so, why might this be the case?
- Are there cultural differences in how scientific misconduct is viewed or handled? Provide an example. What happens to a scientist that is caught in misconduct?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 17.

=== layout: one-panel

# 3. Fabrication, Falsification, and Plagiarism

@body

| Pattern | Example |
| --- | --- |
| Fabrication | Enter measurements for a sample you never ran. |
| Falsification | Delete inconvenient values so the results look stronger. |
| Plagiarism | Copy an explanation without crediting the author. |

@citation

[NSF, 45 CFR 689.1: Research misconduct](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-B/chapter-VI/part-689/section-689.1)

=== layout: one-panel

# 3. One Image, Two Experiments

@body

- A scientist labels one micrograph as results from two separate experiments.
- Compare the raw files with the published figure. Ask whether the error changes the result.
- A correction or retraction changes the scientific record. A misconduct finding requires an investigation.

@citation

[COPE, Retraction guidelines](https://doi.org/10.24318/cope.2019.1.4)  |  [NSF, 45 CFR 689.1: Research misconduct](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-B/chapter-VI/part-689/section-689.1)

=== layout: one-panel

# 3. Hwang Woo-suk and Fabricated Stem-cell Results

@body

- Reports claiming patient-matched human stem-cell lines were retracted after an investigation.
- The stem-cell claims and the separate evidence for cloning a dog are different findings.
- The investigation also raised questions about how human eggs were obtained.

@citation

[Seoul National University, Hwang investigation (2006)](https://en.snu.ac.kr/snunow/snu_media/news?bbsidx=71497&md=v)

=== layout: one-panel

# 3. The Duplicated Panel Was an Honest Mistake

@body

The author finds both originals. The same file was pasted twice during layout.

- Publish the correct panel and explain whether the conclusion changes.
- An honest error can damage a paper. The federal misconduct definition excludes honest error.

@citation

[NSF, 45 CFR 689.1: Research misconduct](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-B/chapter-VI/part-689/section-689.1)  |  [COPE, Retraction guidelines](https://doi.org/10.24318/cope.2019.1.4)

=== layout: two-panels

# 3. The Dog and the Stem Cells

@left

*Snuppy*

DNA tests supported the claim that the dog was a clone.

@right

*Stem cells*

The investigation found no support for the claimed patient-matched stem cell lines. What would a match have looked like?

@citation

[Seoul National University, Hwang investigation (2006)](https://en.snu.ac.kr/snunow/snu_media/news?bbsidx=71497&md=v)

=== layout: one-panel

# 3. Does an Unusual Value Belong in the Data?

@body

Two students measure the same sample. One result looks much higher than expected.

- A documented instrument failure can justify excluding a measurement under a consistent rule.
- Deleting a valid measurement solely because it weakens the conclusion misrepresents the record. What record would let you tell which occurred?

@citation

[NSF, 45 CFR 689.1: Research misconduct](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-B/chapter-VI/part-689/section-689.1)
